I don’t have a live Rust compiler in this chat interface, so **I cannot literally run `rustc` for you right now**. However, I can give you a complete, copy-pasteable implementation that uses `Rc<RefCell<Node>>` (shared ownership + interior mutability) specifically to avoid the lifetime-annotation hell you’re hitting. It uses `Weak` references for back-links so you don’t leak memory with cycles.

If you want to verify instantly, save this to `main.rs` and run:
```bash
rustc main.rs && ./main
```

Here is the robust doubly-linked list / small reference graph. Note that **there are no explicit lifetime annotations on the nodes**—`Rc` handles ownership at runtime, and `RefCell` allows mutation through shared references.

```rust
use std::cell::RefCell;
use std::rc::{Rc, Weak};

/// Shared reference to a node.
type Link = Rc<RefCell<Node>>;

pub struct Node {
    pub value: i32,
    /// Forward reference: shared ownership.
    pub next: Option<Link>,
    /// Backward reference: weak to prevent reference cycles.
    pub prev: Option<Weak<RefCell<Node>>>,
}

impl Node {
    pub fn new(value: i32) -> Link {
        Rc::new(RefCell::new(Node {
            value,
            next: None,
            prev: None,
        }))
    }
}

/// Link `right` immediately after `left`.
/// Uses separate `borrow_mut()` calls on different `RefCell`s, so there is no overlap.
pub fn append(left: Link, right: Link) {
    // Defensive: don't link a node to itself.
    if Rc::ptr_eq(&left, &right) {
        return;
    }

    // Set back-reference on `right` (Weak so cycles don't leak).
    right.borrow_mut().prev = Some(Rc::downgrade(&left));
    // Set forward-reference on `left`.
    left.borrow_mut().next = Some(Rc::clone(&right));
}

/// Traverse forward (1 -> 2 -> 3 ...).
pub fn traverse_forward(start: Link) {
    let mut current = Some(start);
    while let Some(node) = current {
        print!("{} ", node.borrow().value);
        // Clone the Rc reference; the borrow ends before the loop iterates.
        current = node.borrow().next.clone();
    }
    println!();
}

/// Traverse backward (3 -> 2 -> 1 ...).
pub fn traverse_backward(start: Link) {
    let mut current = Some(start);
    while let Some(node) = current {
        print!("{} ", node.borrow().value);
        // Upgrade the Weak pointer back to an Rc if it still exists.
        current = node.borrow().prev.as_ref().and_then(|w| w.upgrade());
    }
    println!();
}

/// Example of safe mutation through shared references.
/// We clone an Rc (cheap) and mutate the underlying node.
pub fn increment(node: Link) {
    node.borrow_mut().value += 1;
}

fn main() {
    let a = Node::new(1);
    let b = Node::new(2);
    let c = Node::new(3);

    // Build 1 <-> 2 <-> 3
    append(a.clone(), b.clone());
    append(b.clone(), c.clone());

    println!("Forward:");
    traverse_forward(a.clone());

    println!("Backward:");
    traverse_backward(c.clone());

    // Mutate safely through one of our shared handles.
    increment(b.clone());

    println!("After incrementing b:");
    traverse_forward(a.clone()); // 1 3 3
}

```

### Why this fixes your borrow-checker pain

| Approach | Why it fails / works |
|----------|----------------------|
| Raw references (`&Node`) | You need lifetimes everywhere (`'a`, `'b`), and you can’t have two mutable references to linked nodes at once. |
| `Box<Node>` | You can’t have back-references easily because ownership is unique and linear. |
| `Rc<RefCell<Node>>` | **Works.** `Rc` allows many owners; `RefCell` allows mutable borrows at runtime (checked). No lifetime annotations on the struct fields are needed. |
| `Weak` for `prev` | If both directions were `Rc`, you’d create a reference cycle and leak memory. `Weak` breaks the cycle but still lets you traverse backward with `.upgrade()`. |

