### Pattern B: `Rc<RefCell>` Shared Graph

If nodes must live outside the container (e.g., passed around freely), use shared ownership with interior mutability. Note: `RefCell` moves borrow-checking to **runtime**; it avoids compile-time lifetime errors but can panic if you borrow mutably twice at runtime.

```rust
use std::cell::RefCell;
use std::rc::{Rc, Weak};

#[derive(Debug)]
pub struct Node {
    pub value: String,
    // Strong references for outgoing edges
    pub edges: Vec<Rc<RefCell<Node>>>,
    // Weak back-reference (optional, prevents cycles)
    pub parent: Option<Weak<RefCell<Node>>>,
}

impl Node {
    pub fn new(value: impl Into<String>) -> Self {
        Node {
            value: value.into(),
            edges: Vec::new(),
            parent: None,
        }
    }
}

pub struct SharedGraph {
    // We keep handles here mainly for root tracking
    pub nodes: Vec<Rc<RefCell<Node>>>,
}

impl SharedGraph {
    pub fn new() -> Self {
        SharedGraph { nodes: Vec::new() }
    }

    pub fn spawn(&mut self, value: impl Into<String>) -> Rc<RefCell<Node>> {
        let node = Rc::new(RefCell::new(Node::new(value)));
        self.nodes.push(node.clone());
        node
    }

    /// Link two nodes via Rc handles. No lifetime annotations needed.
    pub fn connect(
        a: Rc<RefCell<Node>>,
        b: Rc<RefCell<Node>>,
    ) {
        a.borrow_mut().edges.push(b.clone());
        b.borrow_mut().edges.push(a.clone());
    }

    /// Mutate safely through an Rc handle.
    pub fn update(node: Rc<RefCell<Node>>, new_val: String) {
        node.borrow_mut().value = new_val;
    }
}

fn main() {
    let mut g = SharedGraph::new();
    let n1 = g.spawn("Node 1");
    let n2 = g.spawn("Node 2");

    SharedGraph::connect(n1.clone(), n2.clone());

    // Mutate through the clone—no borrow checker involved.
    SharedGraph::update(n1.clone(), "Updated!".to_string());

    println!("{} -> edges: {}",
        n1.borrow().value,
        n1.borrow().edges.len()
    );
}
```

**Caveat:** `borrow_mut()` panics if there's already an active borrow. For a simple graph this is manageable; for complex mutation patterns, Pattern A (arena) is safer because all checks happen at compile time with no runtime borrow panics.

