## Option 1: Arena Graph (Recommended – zero mess)

This is the most robust pattern for complex mutable graphs in Rust. Mutation is trivial because nodes are just indices into a vector.

```rust
#[derive(Debug, Clone, Default)]
pub struct NodeData {
    pub value: String,
    pub edges: Vec<usize>, // indices of neighbors
}

#[derive(Debug, Clone, Default)]
pub struct ArenaGraph {
    nodes: Vec<NodeData>,
}

impl ArenaGraph {
    pub fn new() -> Self {
        Self::default()
    }

    /// Creates a node and returns its index.
    pub fn add_node(&mut self, value: impl Into<String>) -> usize {
        let id = self.nodes.len();
        self.nodes.push(NodeData {
            value: value.into(),
            edges: Vec::new(),
        });
        id
    }

    /// Adds a directed edge: from -> to
    pub fn add_edge(&mut self, from: usize, to: usize) {
        if let (Some(src), Some(_)) = (self.nodes.get(from), self.nodes.get(to)) {
            // We only need to check bounds once; we can push safely
            self.nodes[from].edges.push(to);
        }
    }

    /// Safe mutable access
    pub fn node_mut(&mut self, id: usize) -> Option<&mut NodeData> {
        self.nodes.get_mut(id)
    }

    /// Safe immutable access
    pub fn node(&self, id: usize) -> Option<&NodeData> {
        self.nodes.get(id)
    }
}

fn main() {
    let mut g = ArenaGraph::new();
    let a = g.add_node("A");
    let b = g.add_node("B");
    let c = g.add_node("C");

    g.add_edge(a, b);
    g.add_edge(b, c);
    g.add_edge(a, c);

    // Mutate freely
    if let Some(node) = g.node_mut(a) {
        node.value = "Alpha".to_string();
    }

    // Traverse freely
    println!("Edges from {}:", g.node(a).unwrap().value);
    for &target in &g.node(a).unwrap().edges {
        println!("  -> {}", g.node(target).unwrap().value);
    }
}
```

**Why this works:** There are no reference lifetimes because `edges` stores `usize`, not `&Node`. The borrow checker is happy, and mutation never conflicts.

