### Option 1: `Rc<RefCell>` Doubly-Linked List (True Pointers)

This eliminates all lifetime annotations (`'a`) because `Rc` owns the data on the heap. Mutation works through `.borrow_mut()`.

```rust
use std::cell::RefCell;
use std::rc::Rc;

struct Node {
    value: i32,
    prev: Option<Rc<RefCell<Node>>>,
    next: Option<Rc<RefCell<Node>>>,
}

pub struct LinkedList {
    head: Option<Rc<RefCell<Node>>>,
    tail: Option<Rc<RefCell<Node>>>,
}

impl LinkedList {
    pub fn new() -> Self {
        LinkedList { head: None, tail: None }
    }

    /// Append to the tail. Safe because we only borrow the old tail.
    pub fn push_back(&mut self, value: i32) {
        let node = Rc::new(RefCell::new(Node {
            value,
            prev: self.tail.clone(),
            next: None,
        }));

        match self.tail.take() {
            Some(old_tail) => {
                old_tail.borrow_mut().next = Some(node.clone());
                self.tail = Some(node);
            }
            None => {
                self.head = Some(node.clone());
                self.tail = Some(node);
            }
        }
    }

    /// Remove from the tail. Note: we borrow `old_tail` for value/prev,
    /// then borrow the *new* tail to clear its `next`. These are distinct Rcs,
    /// so this is safe, but ordering matters.
    pub fn pop_back(&mut self) -> Option<i32> {
        let old_tail = self.tail.take()?;
        let value = old_tail.borrow().value;
        let prev = old_tail.borrow().prev.clone();

        self.tail = prev.clone();

        if let Some(ref new_tail) = self.tail {
            new_tail.borrow_mut().next = None;
        } else {
            self.head = None;
        }

        Some(value)
    }
}

fn main() {
    let mut list = LinkedList::new();
    list.push_back(10);
    list.push_back(20);
    list.push_back(30);

    println!("Popped: {:?}", list.pop_back()); // 30
    println!("Popped: {:?}", list.pop_back()); // 20
}
```

**Warning with this approach:** If you try to remove a node from the *middle* of the list, you'll need to borrow both the previous node (`prev`) and the next node (`next`) simultaneously to rewire them. If you do this in the wrong order (e.g., holding a borrow on `prev` while trying to borrow `next` through a different path), `RefCell` will panic at **runtime**. This is why the logic feels "messy"—you're manually managing borrow ordering.

