### Option 2: Arena / Index-Based Graph (Recommended)

For anything more complex than a list (graphs, bidirectional edges, arbitrary mutations), avoid pointer chasing entirely. Store nodes in a `Vec`, reference them by `usize` index, and use `RefCell<Vec<Node>>` so you can borrow the entire container safely.

This has **zero** explicit lifetime annotations and **zero** risk of `RefCell` borrow panics between individual nodes, because you never borrow two nodes at once—only the container.

```rust
use std::cell::RefCell;

#[derive(Debug, Clone, Default)]
pub struct Node {
    pub value: String,
    pub edges: Vec<usize>, // indices into the arena
}

pub struct Graph {
    nodes: RefCell<Vec<Node>>,
}

impl Graph {
    pub fn new() -> Self {
        Graph {
            nodes: RefCell::new(Vec::new()),
        }
    }

    /// Add a node; returns its index (the "reference").
    pub fn add_node(&self, value: impl Into<String>) -> usize {
        let mut nodes = self.nodes.borrow_mut();
        let idx = nodes.len();
        nodes.push(Node {
            value: value.into(),
            edges: Vec::new(),
        });
        idx
    }

    /// Create an edge by index. No lifetime issues, no pointer aliasing.
    pub fn link(&self, from: usize, to: usize) {
        let mut nodes = self.nodes.borrow_mut();
        if from < nodes.len() && to < nodes.len() {
            nodes[from].edges.push(to);
            // For undirected: nodes[to].edges.push(from);
        }
    }

    /// Read a node's value safely.
    pub fn get_value(&self, idx: usize) -> Option<String> {
        self.nodes.borrow().get(idx).map(|n| n.value.clone())
    }

    /// Mutate a specific node by index.
    pub fn set_value(&self, idx: usize, value: impl Into<String>) {
        let mut nodes = self.nodes.borrow_mut();
        if let Some(node) = nodes.get_mut(idx) {
            node.value = value.into();
        }
    }
}

fn main() {
    let graph = Graph::new();
    let a = graph.add_node("A");
    let b = graph.add_node("B");
    let c = graph.add_node("C");

    graph.link(a, b);
    graph.link(b, c);
    graph.link(c, a); // cycle is fine—no reference counting issues

    graph.set_value(b, "Updated B");

    println!("{:?}", graph.get_value(a));
    println!("{:?}", graph.get_value(b));
}
```

