Yes — this is absolutely achievable in 100% safe Rust, but the borrow checker fights you because `Rc<RefCell<Node>>` creates shared ownership combined with interior mutability. The trick is to **never hold a borrow across an update to a neighboring pointer**. Clone the `Rc` handles you need, drop every borrow immediately, then borrow again.

Below is a complete, self-contained implementation. It uses only `std::rc::Rc` and `std::cell::RefCell` (no `unsafe`). I have also included a `Drop` impl that drains the list so that internal `prev`/`next` cycles don’t leak memory.

### Key borrow-checker patterns used here
1. **Scope your borrows in small blocks** (`{ ... }`) so they are dropped before you borrow something else.
2. **Clone the `Rc` pointer first**, then borrow the *new* pointer mutably.
3. **Break links before extracting the value** (`prev` → `None`, `next` → `None`), then use `Rc::try_unwrap` to take ownership of the node.

```rust
use std::cell::RefCell;
use std::rc::Rc;

struct Node<T> {
    value: T,
    prev: Option<Rc<RefCell<Node<T>>>>,
    next: Option<Rc<RefCell<Node<T>>>>,
}

pub struct DoublyLinkedList<T> {
    head: Option<Rc<RefCell<Node<T>>>>,
    tail: Option<Rc<RefCell<Node<T>>>>,
    len: usize,
}

impl<T> DoublyLinkedList<T> {
    pub fn new() -> Self {
        Self {
            head: None,
            tail: None,
            len: 0,
        }
    }

    pub fn len(&self) -> usize {
        self.len
    }

    pub fn is_empty(&self) -> bool {
        self.len == 0
    }

    /// Push to the front.
    pub fn push_front(&mut self, value: T) {
        let new_node = Rc::new(RefCell::new(Node {
            value,
            prev: None,
            next: self.head.clone(),
        }));

        if let Some(ref head) = self.head {
            // Borrow old head mutably to point back at new node.
            head.borrow_mut().prev = Some(new_node.clone());
        } else {
            // List was empty: new node is also the tail.
            self.tail = Some(new_node.clone());
        }

        self.head = Some(new_node);
        self.len += 1;
    }

    /// Push to the back.
    pub fn push_back(&mut self, value: T) {
        let new_node = Rc::new(RefCell::new(Node {
            value,
            prev: self.tail.clone(),
            next: None,
        }));

        if let Some(ref tail) = self.tail {
            tail.borrow_mut().next = Some(new_node.clone());
        } else {
            // List was empty: new node is also the head.
            self.head = Some(new_node.clone());
        }

        self.tail = Some(new_node);
        self.len += 1;
    }

    /// Remove and return the front element.
    pub fn pop_front(&mut self) -> Option<T> {
        let head_rc = self.head.take()?;

        // Clone the next pointer in an isolated borrow scope.
        let next_rc = {
            let b = head_rc.borrow();
            b.next.clone()
        };

        // Disconnect the new head's prev pointer (if any).
        if let Some(ref next) = next_rc {
            next.borrow_mut().prev = None;
        } else {
            // List is now empty.
            self.tail = None;
        }

        self.head = next_rc;
        self.len -= 1;

        // Break the old head's links so it can be deallocated.
        {
            let mut b = head_rc.borrow_mut();
            b.prev = None;
            b.next = None;
        }

        // At this point reference count is 1; extract the value.
        Rc::try_unwrap(head_rc)
            .ok()
            .map(|cell| cell.into_inner().value)
    }

    /// Remove and return the back element.
    pub fn pop_back(&mut self) -> Option<T> {
        let tail_rc = self.tail.take()?;

        // Clone the previous pointer in an isolated borrow scope.
        let prev_rc = {
            let b = tail_rc.borrow();
            b.prev.clone()
        };

        // Disconnect the new tail's next pointer (if any).
        if let Some(ref prev) = prev_rc {
            prev.borrow_mut().next = None;
        } else {
            // List is now empty.
            self.head = None;
        }

        self.tail = prev_rc;
        self.len -= 1;

        // Break the old tail's links.
        {
            let mut b = tail_rc.borrow_mut();
            b.prev = None;
            b.next = None;
        }

        Rc::try_unwrap(tail_rc)
            .ok()
            .map(|cell| cell.into_inner().value)
    }

    /// Drain the list (used by Drop to avoid leaks from cycles).
    pub fn clear(&mut self) {
        while self.pop_back().is_some() {}
    }
}

impl<T> Drop for DoublyLinkedList<T> {
    fn drop(&mut self) {
        // Manually drain so internal prev/next cycles don't leak.
        self.clear();
    }
}

impl<T> Default for DoublyLinkedList<T> {
    fn default() -> Self {
        Self::new()
    }
}

fn main() {
    let mut list = DoublyLinkedList::new();
    list.push_back(10);
    list.push_front(5);
    list.push_back(20);
    list.push_front(1);

    println!("Length: {}", list.len()); // 4

    // Pop from both ends to show it works safely.
    println!("Pop front: {:?}", list.pop_front()); // Some(1)
    println!("Pop back:  {:?}", list.pop_back());  // Some(20)
    println!("Pop front: {:?}", list.pop_front()); // Some(5)
    println!("Pop back:  {:?}", list.pop_back());  // Some(10)
    println!("Empty? {}", list.is_empty());        // true
}
```

