```rust
use std::cell::RefCell;
use std::rc::Rc;

pub struct Node {
    pub id: u32,
    pub value: String,
    // References to other nodes. Because these are Rc, they can form cycles.
    pub edges: Vec<Rc<RefCell<Node>>>,
}

impl Node {
    /// Create a new node wrapped in shared, mutable ownership.
    pub fn new(id: u32, value: impl Into<String>) -> Rc<RefCell<Self>> {
        Rc::new(RefCell::new(Node {
            id,
            value: value.into(),
            edges: Vec::new(),
        }))
    }

    /// Add a directed edge. We clone the Rc so ownership is shared.
    pub fn add_edge(from: &Rc<RefCell<Node>>, to: &Rc<RefCell<Node>>) {
        from.borrow_mut().edges.push(Rc::clone(to));
    }

    /// Read a node and its neighbors safely.
    pub fn print_neighbors(node: &Rc<RefCell<Node>>) {
        // Immutable borrow of this node.
        let borrowed = node.borrow();
        println!(
            "Node {} (value={:?}) has {} outgoing edges:",
            borrowed.id,
            borrowed.value,
            borrowed.edges.len()
        );

        // We can borrow each neighbor immutably because they are separate RefCells.
        for target in &borrowed.edges {
            let t = target.borrow();
            println!("  -> node {} ({:?})", t.id, t.value);
        }
        // `borrowed` is dropped here automatically.
    }

    /// Mutate the value of a node safely, even if other parts of the graph point to it.
    pub fn set_value(node: &Rc<RefCell<Node>>, new_value: impl Into<String>) {
        node.borrow_mut().value = new_value.into();
    }
}

fn main() {
    // Build nodes
    let a = Node::new(1, "A");
    let b = Node::new(2, "B");
    let c = Node::new(3, "C");

    // Wire the graph (can include cycles)
    Node::add_edge(&a, &b);
    Node::add_edge(&a, &c);
    Node::add_edge(&b, &c);
    Node::add_edge(&c, &a); // cycle back to A

    // Mutate safely through one of the shared handles
    Node::set_value(&b, "B-modified");

    // Read through the shared handle `a`
    Node::print_neighbors(&a);
}
```

### Why this avoids your specific errors
- **No lifetimes:** Everything lives in `Rc` boxes on the heap. The compiler never needs to prove stack-frame exclusivity.
- **No overlapping compile-time borrows:** `borrow_mut()` and `borrow()` are runtime checks inside `RefCell`. The compiler is happy because `RefCell` hides the mutability behind a runtime contract.

### The trade-off (important)
Because this is safe Rust with cycles, borrow conflicts become **runtime panics**, not compile errors. If you accidentally do this:

```rust
let mut a_mut = a.borrow_mut();
let b_ref = a.borrow(); // PANIC at runtime: already borrowed mutably
```

…your program will abort. So you must ensure you don’t hold a mutable borrow across an operation that borrows the same (or cyclically related) node. The example above avoids this by dropping `borrowed` before doing anything nested.

### If you want zero runtime borrow-checking overhead
For large graphs, the “industry” approach in Rust is to avoid references altogether and use **indices into a `Vec`**:

```rust
struct Graph {
    nodes: Vec<NodeData>,
}
struct NodeData {
    value: String,
    neighbors: Vec<usize>, // just indices, not pointers
}
```

This has no `Rc`, no `RefCell`, no lifetimes, and no runtime borrow panics, but nodes don’t hold “references” in the pointer sense.

If you copy the `Rc<RefCell>` code above, it is structurally sound and uses only standard-library patterns that compile without lifetime annotations. Just be careful about nested `borrow_mut()` calls across cycles.

