### Option 2: Doubly-Linked List with `Rc<RefCell>` + `Weak`

If you specifically need a true linked structure (e.g., for an algorithm that expects pointer-like nodes), use `Rc` for shared ownership, `RefCell` for interior mutability, and `Weak` for the backward link to prevent reference cycles.

```rust
use std::cell::RefCell;
use std::rc::{Rc, Weak};

pub struct ListNode {
    pub value: i32,
    pub next: Option<Rc<RefCell<ListNode>>>,
    pub prev: Option<Weak<RefCell<ListNode>>>,
}

impl ListNode {
    pub fn new(value: i32) -> Rc<RefCell<Self>> {
        Rc::new(RefCell::new(ListNode {
            value,
            next: None,
            prev: None,
        }))
    }
}

pub struct DoublyLinkedList {
    head: Option<Rc<RefCell<ListNode>>>,
    tail: Option<Rc<RefCell<ListNode>>>,
}

impl DoublyLinkedList {
    pub fn new() -> Self {
        DoublyLinkedList { head: None, tail: None }
    }

    pub fn push_back(&mut self, value: i32) {
        let new_node = ListNode::new(value);
        match self.tail.take() {
            Some(old_tail) => {
                old_tail.borrow_mut().next = Some(Rc::clone(&new_node));
                new_node.borrow_mut().prev = Some(Rc::downgrade(&old_tail));
                self.tail = Some(new_node);
            }
            None => {
                self.head = Some(Rc::clone(&new_node));
                self.tail = Some(new_node);
            }
        }
    }

    pub fn pop_back(&mut self) -> Option<i32> {
        self.tail.take().map(|old_tail| {
            // Break the back-link first
            if let Some(prev_weak) = old_tail.borrow_mut().prev.take() {
                if let Some(prev_rc) = prev_weak.upgrade() {
                    prev_rc.borrow_mut().next = None;
                    self.tail = Some(prev_rc);
                } else {
                    // Previous node was dropped; this was the only node.
                    self.tail = None;
                    self.head = None;
                }
            } else {
                // This was the head (only node).
                self.head = None;
            }
            // Borrow is finished by now; safe to read value.
            old_tail.borrow().value
        })
    }

    pub fn len(&self) -> usize {
        let mut count = 0;
        let mut current = self.head.clone();
        while let Some(node) = current {
            count += 1;
            current = node.borrow().next.clone();
        }
        count
    }
}

fn main() {
    let mut list = DoublyLinkedList::new();
    list.push_back(1);
    list.push_back(2);
    list.push_back(3);

    assert_eq!(list.pop_back(), Some(3));
    assert_eq!(list.pop_back(), Some(2));
    assert_eq!(list.len(), 1);
}
```

**Key points that prevent borrow-checker errors:**
- `prev` is `Weak`, not `Rc`, so you don’t create an unbreakable cycle (`head <-> ... <-> tail`).
- `borrow_mut()` is always on a specific `Rc`, never overlapping with another borrow of the same `RefCell`.
- `old_tail.borrow().value` happens after the `borrow_mut()` scope ends.

