### If you need a more complex "graph" (not just a list)

For arbitrary graphs with many interconnections, `Rc<RefCell>` can become messy and risk runtime borrow panics if you're not careful. A more robust and scalable pattern is an **arena/index-based graph**. Instead of nodes pointing to each other with pointers, nodes live in a `Vec` and point to each other via `usize` indices:

```rust
use std::collections::HashMap;

pub struct ArenaGraph {
    nodes: Vec<NodeData>,
    edges: Vec<Vec<usize>>, // adjacency list: node -> [target indices]
}

struct NodeData {
    value: String,
    metadata: HashMap<String, String>,
}

impl ArenaGraph {
    pub fn new() -> Self {
        Self { nodes: Vec::new(), edges: Vec::new() }
    }

    pub fn add_node(&mut self, value: String) -> usize {
        let id = self.nodes.len();
        self.nodes.push(NodeData { value, metadata: HashMap::new() });
        self.edges.push(Vec::new());
        id
    }

    pub fn add_edge(&mut self, from: usize, to: usize) {
        if from < self.edges.len() && to < self.nodes.len() {
            self.edges[from].push(to);
        }
    }

    pub fn get_node_mut(&mut self, id: usize) -> Option<&mut NodeData> {
        self.nodes.get_mut(id)
    }

    pub fn neighbors(&self, id: usize) -> Option<&Vec<usize>> {
        self.edges.get(id)
    }
}

fn main() {
    let mut g = ArenaGraph::new();
    let a = g.add_node("A".into());
    let b = g.add_node("B".into());
    g.add_edge(a, b);
    g.add_edge(b, a);
    g.get_node_mut(a).unwrap().metadata.insert("key".into(), "val".into());
}
```

This version has **zero lifetime annotations**, **zero `RefCell` runtime panics**, and allows arbitrary mutation because you're borrowing from a `Vec` by index, not through inter-object references.

